<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saladin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1193">1193</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Salah al-Din Yusuf ibn Ayyub, Salah al-Din · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saladin (Salah al-Din Yusuf ibn Ayyub) was a Kurdish Muslim military leader and the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire</a>, who died in 1193. He became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> of Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 1169, ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a> and united Egypt and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> under his rule. His victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin">Battle of Hattin</a> in 1187 and recapture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> made him a celebrated figure, though accounts of the Ayyubid Empire note that his division of territory among relatives prepared the empire's fragmentation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saladin.jpg" alt="Saladin" width="320" height="301" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group (CNG) · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al-Nasir_I_Salah_al-Din_Yusuf_(Saladin)._AH_564-589_(1169-1193_CE)_%C3%86_Dirham_(30.1mm,_13.28_g,_6h)._Without_mint-name._Dated_AH_586_(AD_1190-91)._Sultan_sitting_facing,_cross-legged,_on_high-backed_throne_(obverse).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Saladin was born about 1137 in Tikrit, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. His personal name was Yusuf; Salah ad-Din is an honorific meaning Righteousness of the Faith. His parents were of Kurdish Rawadiya ancestry, a tribe originally of Arab descent that had become Kurdicized, and his father Najm al-Din Ayyub was a Kurdish mercenary. His military career began under his paternal uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirkuh">Shirkuh</a>, a commander in the service of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-al-din">Nur al-Din</a>. In 1164, aged 26, Saladin went with Shirkuh to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> to help restore the vizier Shawar, though his role in that expedition was minor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirkuh">Shirkuh</a> and Saladin returned in 1169, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amalric-i-of-jerusalem">Amalric I of Jerusalem</a> besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>; Shirkuh became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> and died the same year, and the Fatimid caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-adid">al-Adid</a> appointed Saladin. In 1171, after al-Adid's death, the Abbasid khutba was pronounced in Cairo, ending the Isma'ili Fatimid Caliphate and returning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> to the Sunni Abbasid caliph. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-al-din">Nur al-Din</a> died in 1174, Saladin peacefully entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hama">Hama</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Homs</a>, defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zengid-dynasty">Zengids</a> at the Battle of the Horns of Hama in 1175 and was proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of Egypt and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> by the Abbasid caliph. By 1182 he had taken <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, though he failed to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>, and he escaped two attempts on his life by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-assassins">Order of Assassins</a>.</p><p>On 25 November, at Montgisard near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramla">Ramla</a>, a small Templar force surprised his army and he fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. His army defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin">Battle of Hattin</a> in 1187, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> capitulated on 2 October 1187. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balian-of-ibelin">Balian of Ibelin</a> threatened to kill the Muslim hostages and destroy the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dome-of-the-rock">Dome of the Rock</a> if quarter were refused, and Saladin accepted terms allowing ransom. About 18,000 poorer citizens were ransomed through a collection, while another 15,000 were enslaved.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin">Hattin</a> and the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> prompted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-crusade">Third Crusade</a> (1189 to 1192), partly financed by a special Saladin tithe in 1188. Saladin died of a fever at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> on 4 March 1193, having given away most of his wealth; he was buried in a mausoleum beside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-mosque">Umayyad Mosque</a>. Medieval Europeans held him up as a model of kingship, and Dante placed him among the virtuous non-Christians in limbo. His reputation had been largely forgotten in the Muslim world, eclipsed by figures such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a>, until the 19th century, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-scott">Walter Scott</a>'s romantic portrayal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii">Wilhelm II</a>'s visit to his tomb in 1898 helped to make him a hero of the struggle against the West in the Arab world.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Saladin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Saladin</b>'s nephew, Sultan al-Kamil, later handed Jerusalem to Emperor Frederick II by treaty in 1229, in return for help against his own Ayyubid relatives.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Al-Azhar, developed as a teaching institution from 988 onwards, became the most important centre of Islamic learning in the Arab world, a status it retains today although <b>Saladin</b> reversed its Ismaili orientation after 1171.</blockquote><a href="/empire/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom won important victories, including King Baldwin IV’s defeat of <b>Saladin</b> at the Battle of Montgisard in 1177, and King Amalric I invaded Egypt several times between 1163 and 1169, briefly forcing it to pay tribute.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Relations with Egypt worsened under the Ayyubids: after a Nubian attack on Aswan, Shams al-Dawla Turan-Shah, brother of <b>Saladin</b>, sacked Qasr Ibrim in 1172–1173.</blockquote><a href="/empire/makuria">Makuria · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…Baldwin I of Jerusalem and Sigurd I of Norway took it after a siege and made it the centre of the Lordship of Sidon; <b>Saladin</b> captured it in 1187, German crusaders restored it in 1197, the Ayyubids destroyed it in 1249 and the Mongols under Kitbuqa in 1260.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sidon">Sidon · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>When <b>Saladin</b> took Amid from the Zengid coalition in April 1183, the Ayyubids counted among its stores 80,000 candles, a tower full of arrowheads and 1,040,000 books.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zengid-dynasty">Zengid dynasty · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tikrit Castle, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.6,43.69&amp;year=1183" aria-label="Tikrit Castle on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Saladin"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5955 4550h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5955 4550h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.6,43.69&amp;year=1183">What was here in 1183? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
